I need to look into this further. Let me check the details.

The keydown for a mapped key calls unlockAudio().

Next, write the M4 browser check. HUD pixel sampling is done via canvas's `getImageData` (the page's canvas has an alpha:false 2d context — reading with a separate getContext('2d') call returns the same context, so no problem — main.mjs calls getContext('2d', {alpha:false}); a subsequent getContext('2d') returns the same context object → getImageData works, as was done in the previous check).

Score region: x 119..199, y 2..20. Shield bar: x 308..312, y 100..199. Weapon icon: x 286..304, y 2..20.

Orange shield color: palette 74. What RGB? Check the palette — the previous screenshot showed an orange bar. Instead of hardcoding the RGB, count the non-black pixels in the bar region (100 rows filled → about 400 non-black px out of 4×100; when shield is 100). After damage: the filled count decreases.

Score liveness: after killing an enemy, the score digits change. Compare the pixel diff in the score region before and after.

Audio probe script (injected before page load):
```js
(() => {
  window.__ap = { ctx: null, starts: [], state: 'none' };
  const OrigAC = window.AudioContext || window.webkitAudioContext;
  if (!OrigAC) return;
  const Wrapped = function(...a) {
    const c = new OrigAC(...a);
    window.__ap.ctx = c;
    const st = () => { window.__ap.state = c.state; };
    st();
    c.addEventListener('statechange', st);
    return c;
  };
  Wrapped.prototype = OrigAC.prototype;
  Wrapped.name = 'AudioContext';
  window.AudioContext = Wrapped;
  window.webkitAudioContext = Wrapped;
  const origStart = AudioBufferSourceNode.prototype.start;
  AudioBufferSourceNode.prototype.start = function(...a) {
    try {
      const b = this.buffer;
      if (b) {
        const ch = b.getChannelData(0);
        let nz = 0, mx = 0;
        for (let i = 0; i < ch.length; i += 4) { const v = ch[i]; if (v) nz++; const av = v < 0 ? -v : v; if (av > mx) mx = av; }
        window.__ap.starts.push({ len: b.length, rate: b.sampleRate, nz, mx });
      }
    } catch (e) { /* ignore */ }
    return origStart.apply(this, a);
  };
})();
```
Note: the probe must be installed before the page's own scripts run — use Page.addScriptToEvaluateOnNewDocument.

Flow:
1. New target with the probe script.
2. Wait for ready.
3. keydown ArrowLeft (gesture) → unlockAudio → context is created, resume → 'running' (headless: AudioContext resume works; the previous session verified 'running' after a gesture).
4. Check __raptor.audio.contextState === 'running'.
5. Record sfx0; hold Space for 1.2 seconds → sfxN > sfx0 (firing SFX counted).
6. Probe: __ap.starts.length ≥ 1; the first start has len>500, rate within the range [8000,48000], nz>50, mx>0.02 → actual GUN_FX data is scheduled. (GUN_FX: s8 PCM; verify that it's non-trivial.)
   Also verify that the buffer comes from the GLB: len/rate match what was decoded — this is inherently true from the code path (bufferFor reads the GLB). The probe proves that an actual AudioBuffer with real samples is being played.
7. HUD initial pixels: count of score region, count of shield bar, count of weapon icon.
8. Fire sweep for up to 30 seconds until hud.score > initial score (enemy killed → score up). Then: hud.score > score0; pixel diff in score region > 0; hud.shields === the energy number (via hud field only — can't read game.energy from the page... __raptor.hud.shields is that value).
9. Hold ArrowUp for 20 seconds (no firing) → hud.shields < shieldBefore (hazard zone damage); shield bar fill count decreased; player is alive (100→about 60-80).
10. Also confirm that r.audio.sfxCount increased during this (hit SFX FX.HIT) — a nice extra proof that sfxCount tracks actual SFX events.

Time: startup ~5s + firing 1.5s + sweep ≤30s + UP 20s ≈ ≤60s.

Write tools/.tmp-m4-browser.mjs.

